<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walter Rodney</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Walter Anthony Rodney</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walter Rodney was a Guyanese historian, political activist and academic, born into a working-class family in Georgetown. After a PhD in African History in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1966, he taught at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> in Tanzania; his ban from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in 1968 set off protests known as the Rodney riots. His How <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> Underdeveloped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> (1972) argued that exploitation by European imperialists led directly to the modern underdevelopment of most of the continent. A Pan-Africanist and Marxist, he joined the Working People's Alliance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a> and was killed by an explosive device in his car in 1980; a Commission of Inquiry concluded it had been a state-ordered killing.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Walter Anthony Rodney was born on 23 March 1942 into a working-class family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgetown-guyana">Georgetown, Guyana</a>. He attended the University College of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> from 1960 and took a first-class honours degree in history in 1963. In 1966, at 24, he earned a PhD in African History at the School of Oriental and African Studies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. His dissertation on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> on the Upper Guinea Coast was published by Oxford University Press in 1970 as A History of the Upper Guinea Coast 1545–1800 and was acclaimed for its originality in challenging the conventional view.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He taught at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> in Tanzania in 1966–67 and from 1969 to 1974, and in 1968 at the University of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> at Mona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. He argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> could break from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> only under the banner of socialism and the leadership of the working classes. On 15 October 1968 the government of Prime Minister Hugh Shearer of Jamaica declared him persona non grata and the university dismissed him. Protests by students and the poor of West Kingston escalated into the Rodney riots, which began on 16 October and left six dead and heavy damage.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> he rose to associate professor in 1973, supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a>’s socialist government and spoke to villagers in Kiswahili. How Europe Underdeveloped Africa (1972) described how Africa had been exploited by European imperialists, which he argued led directly to the modern underdevelopment of most of the continent. It became influential and controversial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1974 Rodney returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a>, but the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbes-burnham">Forbes Burnham</a> rescinded the promised professorship at the University of Guyana. He joined the Working People’s Alliance, which sought to replace ethnic politics with class solidarity. In 1979 he was arrested and charged with arson after two government offices burned; the charges were dropped for lack of evidence.</p><p>On 13 June 1980 he was killed in Georgetown, aged 38, by an explosive device in his car. His brother Donald, injured in the blast, said a Guyana Defence Force sergeant, Gregory Smith, had given Walter the device. A Commission of Inquiry concluded that it was a state-ordered killing and that Burnham must have known of the plot. Donald, convicted in 1982 of possessing explosives, was exonerated on appeal on 14 April 2021.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a> awarded Rodney its Order of Excellence in 1993 and set up a Walter Rodney Chair in History. The American Historical Association gave him the Albert J. Beveridge Award in 1982 for A History of the Guyanese Working People, 1881–1905.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Walter Rodney” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…further: Frantz Fanon in The Wretched of the Earth (1961), the Senegalese scholar Cheikh Anta Diop, who argued in Nations nègres et culture (1954) that ancient Egypt had been a Black African civilisation, a much-debated thesis, and the Guyanese historian <b>Walter Rodney</b> in How Europe Underdeveloped Africa (1972).</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>…and Un-British Rule in India (1901) that British rule drained India’s wealth through charges and profits sent home, and <b>Walter Rodney</b>’s How Europe Underdeveloped Africa (1972) held that colonialism carried the surplus of African labour to Europe while taking from Africans the power to set their own goals.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1965 Kwame Nkrumah called neocolonialism “the last stage of imperialism”, and <b>Walter Rodney</b>, in How Europe Underdeveloped Africa (1972), described a world divided into exploiters and exploited.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Mercantilist practices appeared in early modern Venice, Genoa and Pisa in the control of the Mediterranean bullion trade, and the Guyanese historian <b>Walter Rodney</b> dated the period from the 15th-century Portuguese and Spanish voyages.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mercantilism">Mercantilism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgetown-guyana">Georgetown</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guyana">Guyana</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgetown-guyana">Georgetown</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guyana">Guyana</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.81,-58.15&amp;year=1966" aria-label="Georgetown on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Walter Rodney"><image href="/maps/globes/g30000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5157 4419h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5157 4419h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.81,-58.15&amp;year=1966">What was here in 1966? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
